Curriculum Learning
The fresh full rerun in `20260406_102744` confirmed that curriculum learning was a decisive algorithmic contribution. The current curriculum-trained MAPPO checkpoint achieved `1.25` mean food deliveries per episode and a delivery conversion rate of `58.3%`, while the weaker older configuration achieved `0.00` deliveries and `0.0%` conversion. This difference was statistically strong (Welch's t-test: `p = 0.000828`, Cohen's `d = 1.254`). The weaker policy could still discover and pick up food, but it failed to complete the full `explore -> discover -> pick up -> return -> deliver` loop reliably. This shows that the curriculum was necessary for stable decentralized task completion.

Baseline Performance
The learned MAPPO swarm policy significantly outperformed both rule-based and random baselines in the same final-stage environment. The learned policy achieved `1.25` mean food deliveries per episode, compared with `0.30` for the rule-based baseline and `0.05` for the random baseline. These differences were statistically significant (two-sample t-test: `p = 0.012250` and `p = 0.001235`, respectively). This supports the conclusion that learned decentralized coordination was stronger than predefined heuristics or stochastic action selection.

Effect of Stigmergy
The strongest evidence for digital pheromone came from the focused repeated-source killer experiment within `20260406_102744`. In this task, a pheromone-trained swarm significantly outperformed a no-pheromone-trained swarm. At `6` agents, the pheromone-trained policy achieved `1.32` mean deliveries, while the no-pheromone-trained control achieved `0.00` (`paired p = 0.00653`). At the new `30`-agent upper bound, the pheromone-trained swarm achieved `2.66` mean deliveries versus `0.00` for the no-pheromone-trained control (`paired p = 0.000847`, Wilcoxon `p = 9.96e-06`). Late-episode deliveries were also significantly higher at `30` agents (`1.12` vs `0.00`, `paired p = 0.01868`). These results support the conclusion that stigmergic communication through digital pheromone is a causal contributor to swarm coordination and scalability.

Swarm Scaling
The rerun also showed strong broad scaling in the normal final-stage environment. Mean food deliveries increased from `0.05` at `1` agent to `1.25` at `6` agents, then continued to rise to `4.65` at `30` agents. Exploration coverage also increased with swarm size. This demonstrates that the learned decentralized policy scales to larger swarms without centralized control. Importantly, the focused killer experiment shows that this scaling is not only due to parallel effort; under repeated-source route reuse, pheromone-mediated coordination contributes significantly to the performance gain.

Robustness
The swarm was evaluated under harder conditions including extra obstacles, sensor noise, and failed agents. Compared with the final-stage baseline of `1.25` mean deliveries, performance remained at `1.05` under sensor noise and `0.70` with two failed agents, showing partial robustness. Performance declined more noticeably in the high-obstacle condition to `0.65`, identifying environmental complexity as a real limitation. These results show that the system is resilient to some disruptions, but obstacle-dense environments remain a challenge for future improvement.

Physical Swarm Deployment
The learned control framework was also transferred onto a physical swarm of robots. In the hardware system, robots use local sensing and decentralized action selection while Mission Control maintains the shared digital pheromone field. This physical deployment demonstrates that the project is not limited to simulation: the learned swarm behavior and stigmergic coordination mechanism can be embodied in real robots, supporting the real-world feasibility of decentralized swarm intelligence.
